                        "review": "[Spoilers throughout]\n\nSince many of the negative reviews mainly complain about the toxic and unlikable characters, I think it would be good to address my issues with those (i.e. both the characters and the complaints about them) first. Now, I do think the game should have appropriate flags and/or a more detailed description so players know what to expect, but I found the actual content, despite its pervasiveness, both mild and unengaging due to the poor execution.\n\nThe main issue is Sir Brash (top notch naming btw), who, from the moment he appears on screen, constantly belittles, insults and sexually harasses you, in addition to very overtly stating that he's looking forward to raping you later. As bad as that may sound (and would be to experience in person), his dialogue is so ridiculously over the top and forcefully edgy that he sounds more like a bad comedian trying to be offensive rather than an actual threat, which, ironically, is supported by the fact that avoiding him is actually very easy if you're simply polite and deferential.\nThe first time he wants to drag you into the woods, you can just say \"Sir, please let go of me.\" firmly, and he'll respect it, which is quite comical. After that, he'll continue to unsubtly imply what he expects from you eventually, but will always back off if you reject him. At some point, he will actually assault you, but again you can escape with a single dialog choice, and I wouldn't describe the scene as particularly disturbing.\n\nIn fact, I found the initial story hook (you get framed for murder, guillotined, and then thrown into prison when you don't die) much more upsetting than any of the characters' behavior afterwards.\n\nAll that said, my main issue with the game was simply its terrible writing. I generally don't expect much more from VNs than from fanfics, but paid content should still have at least a certain level of quality and here, while syntactically clean, the prose is amateurish at best. There is no tension or sense of urgency, and it fails completely to convey the terror and helplessness the main character should realistically feel after her entire life gets pulled out from under her. The dialogue options are also often so stilted and unnatural they feel comically out of place, while the things any sane person would say in such situations are of course not available options.\n\nFinally, the game is way too short for the full price, but that's a minor issue by comparison.",
